# Line endings, normalised in the repository.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. CI builds web/dist on Linux and then runs
# `git diff --exit-code -- web/dist` to prove the committed artefact matches the
# source it was built from. That check compared a freshly built LF file against
# a committed CRLF one and failed on every line — so it reported "web/dist is
# stale" for a dist that was byte-for-byte correct in content, on every push
# since 1.6.1. A guard that cries wolf on every run is a guard nobody reads, and
# this one was guarding the thing that makes the binary serve the right UI.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on the authoring machine was not enough on its own: it is a
# local setting, it only applies to files git decides are text, and it does
# nothing about blobs that were committed before it was set. An attributes file
# is the version-controlled answer and it applies to everyone, including CI.
#
# text=auto lets git detect text and store it with LF; eol=lf checks it out with
# LF everywhere. Editors on Windows have handled LF for many years, and the
# alternative — normalising in the repo but checking out CRLF — puts the working
# tree back in disagreement with what CI builds.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, and explicitly so: git's heuristics are good but a corrupted cover or
# a mangled webp is a silent, unrecoverable kind of wrong, and these never need
# diffing.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.webp   binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.db     binary
*.sqlite binary

# Shell scripts must keep LF regardless of anything above: a CRLF shebang line
# makes the interpreter part of the name and the script simply does not run.
*.sh text eol=lf

# EXPLICIT, not detected. `text=auto` asks git to guess, and git's guess is what
# broke: one stray lone CR in web/dist/index.html (an autocrlf double-conversion
# of an already-CRLF blob, CRLF -> CRCRLF) made it judge the file BINARY, which
# switched normalisation off, which meant the CRLF could never be cleaned up.
# Naming the types removes the guess from the loop.
*.html text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.jsx  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.go   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.svg  text eol=lf
# Locale files (internal/i18n/*.txt, data/Locales/*.txt) are text a translator
# edits, on whatever machine they have. Named for the same reason as the types
# above: the parser normalises CRLF and lone CR itself, but the committed bytes
# should not depend on git's guess about a file full of Bengali.
*.txt  text eol=lf

# THE ONE FILE THAT MUST KEEP ITS CARRIAGE RETURNS. The parser fixture contains a
# CRLF line and a lone-CR line on purpose, because "a file edited on Windows
# parses the same as one edited on Linux" is a promise with no test unless the
# test data actually holds those bytes. `text=auto eol=lf` above would rewrite
# them to LF on commit and the two assertions would pass vacuously for ever.
internal/i18n/testdata/agree.txt -text
